{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Migration research (official guide py.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/migration/ + real-world compat shims codeofaxel/Kiln mcp_compat.py, mcp-hangar _sdk_compat.py): FastMCP was RENAMED to MCPServer at mcp.server.mcpserver (decorator API unchanged). rebar-specific inventory: (1) mcp_server.py:607 constructor passes host/port/streamable_http_path/transport_security — v2 moves ALL transport params off the constructor onto run()/streamable_http_app(); rebar already wraps mcp.streamable_http_app (line 634, ProxyAuthMiddleware) and has a single run() call site (line 719), so the plumbing fits but the wrap must be re-verified against the v2 app-builder signature. (2) mcp.server.auth + mcp.server.transport_security import paths are UNCHANGED in v2; token_verifier/auth kwargs remain. (3) mcp.types stays as a permanent alias of the new mcp-types dist — ToolAnnotations import unchanged; camelCase ATTRIBUTE access (.outputSchema etc.) breaks but rebar only mentions those in docstrings, not code. (4) Gotchas to test: DNS-rebinding auto-allowlist is now host:port so a bare 'Host: localhost' gets 421 (audit tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_http_transport.py + auth tests); lifespan now runs ONCE per process not per session; sync def tools now run on a worker thread; McpError -> MCPError with positional ctor; sse-starlette floor jumps to >=3; pydantic-settings and httpx no longer pulled in by mcp (check for indirect reliance); scripts/gen_mcp_reference.py:72 imports the old path too. Verdict: mostly-mechanical single-change migration (no compat shim needed since we can drop <2 outright), with design REVIEW (not redesign) needed at: streamable_http_app wrap, 421 host-header behavior in tests, lifespan scope, and the JSON-RPC-error-vs-isError semantics for MCPError raised inside tools. Alternative fastmcp (PrefectHQ, v3.4) is a separate ecosystem — not recommended as a migration target; official-SDK MCPServer port is the converged OSS path."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785724216852840001,"uuid":"d99cffdf-1d8e-4f4a-8559-1aa301bced22"}